"Let $O_1,\\dots,O_M$ be the orbits of $\\operatorname{Sym}(n)^2$ under $\\operatorname{Iso}_1(n)$, and let $B_i$ be the corresponding zero-one matrices. Let $\\mathfrak{T}$ denote the Terwilliger algebra of the conjugacy scheme of $\\operatorname{Sym}(n)$, and let $\\mathfrak{B}=\\langle B_i:i=1,\\dots,M\\rangle$. **Equality conjecture.** The matrices $B_i$, $i=1,\\dots,M$, form a basis of $\\mathfrak{T}$; equivalently, as $\\mathbb{C}$-algebras,\n\n$$\n\\mathfrak{T}=\\mathfrak{B}.\n$$\n\nThe preceding discussion establishes the inclusion $\\mathfrak{T}\\subseteq\\mathfrak{B}$, while the reverse inclusion is described as reasonable but substantially more difficult to prove. Thus the equality remains open in the source."